  "result": "I've written the revised record to `record.jsonl`. It only changes the `blind` scenario, which is what made the gate fail. I couldn't test it here, so whether it passes depends on the harness re-run.\n\n**Why `blind` could never come out unknown:** it hid the side-by-side overlap (`rel.longitudinal_overlap`) and expected the rule to report unknown. But the overlap isn't only supplied by the source. Core recomputes it from each car's position, heading and size, and every car this rule checks for lane occupancy must carry those. So hiding the overlap changes nothing, and the scene still comes out violated, exactly as the coder reported.\n\n**The fix:** `blind` now hides `dev.governs` instead, which is which lanes the railway device controls. That relation always comes from the source and is never recomputed. With it hidden, nobody can tell whether stopping is required in the subject's lane, so it can't be decided whether the duty applies. The obligation side is still clearly breached (another car sits beside the subject, same direction, 3 m overlap), so the overall verdict should be unknown, with the hidden atom as the only reason. The scenario's description now says this.\n\n**Why I didn't use the coder's first suggestion (`st.on_way`):** in a two-lane scene, four different lanes can't exist anyway. So the \"four or more lanes\" exception is false whether or not `st.on_way` is observed, and it wouldn't produce unknown. I didn't use the other suggestion, hiding `ru.aggregate`, because hiding it would also make the subject's own \"is a vehicle\" check unknown, not just whether the other car counts as traffic.\n\nThe rest of the record is unchanged: the span mappings, the applicability and obligation text, the parameters, the literal four and the other scenarios. The coder's existing rule and other witnesses can stay. Only the `blind` witness needs rebuilding with `dev.governs` hidden.",
